/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   ZENOFIT: STRENGTH STANDARDS
   Source: strengthlevel.com/strength-standards, retrieved 25 Aug 2026.

   What this is: for 71 lifts, the load (or rep count) that a man or a woman
   of a given bodyweight has to hit to be counted at each of five levels.
   The site's own words for those levels are beginner / novice / intermediate
   / advanced / elite; the app renders them as WOOD · GOLD · DIAMOND ·
   TITANIUM · VIBRANIUM, in that order, and STD_LEVELS below is that order.
   The rename is a display choice and lives only in the labels, and nothing here
   is stored on a user record, so it can be changed without touching data.

   ── The shape ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Every table is on the same two bodyweight grids (STD_BW), 5 kg apart, so
   the bodyweight column is not repeated 12,780 times: each row of `m` / `f`
   is one bodyweight, in grid order, holding the five levels comma-separated
   and rows semicolon-separated. A bodyweight between two rows is read by
   interpolating them (stdRow() in app.js), which is why the grid has to stay
   parallel to the strings, so if you ever regenerate this file, regenerate
   both together.

   `reps: true` marks a bodyweight movement whose standards are REP COUNTS,
   not kilos: pull-ups, push-ups, dips and the rest. A zero in one of those
   is the site's own "<1", meaning the level is below a single rep; the app
   prints it as "<1" rather than "0".

   Everything is in KILOS. The app converts on the way out, because the unit
   on screen is the user's, and never converts on the way in.

   STD_MATCH ties the app's own library to these tables by the stable
   "default-N" id, and only where the movement is genuinely the same lift on
   the same implement, since it exists to pre-fill a number the user could type
   themselves, so a guess is worse than a blank. Reps-measured lifts are
   deliberately absent from it: the log stores a set as reps × weight, and
   there is no honest way to read "your best set of pull-ups at bodyweight"
   back out of that.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

"use strict";

/* low to high, the order every ladder in the UI is drawn in */
const STD_LEVELS = ["wood", "gold", "diamond", "titanium", "vibranium"];

/* the bodyweight (kg) each row of a packed table stands for */
